Below is a **complete, self‑contained program** that shows how C++20 *concepts* can be used to:

1. **Define a reusable concept** (`Addable`).
2. **Constrain a function** (`sum`), a *class template* (`Accumulator`) and even a *generic lambda*.
3. **Trigger a nice, compile‑time error** when a type that does **not** satisfy the concept is used.
4. **Interact with the standard library** (e.g. `std::string` is `Addable` because `operator+` exists).

```cpp
// demo_concepts.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <concepts>
#include <string>

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   1)  Concept definition
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */
// `Addable<T>` is satisfied when `T` supports the binary
// operator `+` that returns a value of the same type.
template<typename T>
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) {
    { a + b } -> std::same_as<T>;
};

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   2)  Function constrained by a concept
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */
template<Addable T>
T sum(const T& a, const T& b) {
    return a + b;               // we are guaranteed that `+` exists
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   3)  Class template constrained by a concept
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */
template<typename T>
requires Addable<T>
class Accumulator {
public:
    Accumulator() : total{} {}                 // default‑construct the total

    void add(const T& value) { total += value; }

    T total_value() const { return total; }

private:
    T total;
};

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   4)  Generic lambda constrained by a concept
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */
auto increment = [](auto x) requires Addable<decltype(x)> {
    return x + x;      // or just return x + 1 if you want a numeric increment
};

/* ----------------------------------------------------------
   5)  Usage
   ---------------------------------------------------------- */
int main()
{
    // 5.1  Using the function
    std::cout << "sum(3, 4) = " << sum(3, 4) << '\n';           // 7

    // 5.2  Using the class template with an integral type
    Accumulator<int> int_acc;
    int_acc.add(10);
    int_acc.add(20);
    std::cout << "Int total = " << int_acc.total_value() << '\n'; // 30

    // 5.3  Using the class template with std::string
    Accumulator<std::string> str_acc;
    str_acc.add("Hello ");
    str_acc.add("world!");
    std::cout << "String total = " << str_acc.total_value() << '\n'; // Hello world!

    // 5.4  Using the generic lambda
    std::cout << "increment(5) = " << increment(5) << '\n';        // 10
    std::cout << "increment(\"Hi\") = " << increment(std::string{"Hi"}) << '\n';

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------
       5.5  What if we use a type that doesn't satisfy the concept?
       ---------------------------------------------------------- */
    // Uncommenting the next lines will cause a nice compile‑time
    // error because `struct NoAdd` has no operator+.

    /*
    struct NoAdd { int x; };
    Accumulator<NoAdd> no_acc;  // <-- compile error
    */

    return 0;
}
```

### What to compile

```bash
g++ -std=c++20 demo_concepts.cpp -o demo_concepts
./demo_concepts
```

### Why this is a good illustration

| Feature | How it’s shown |
|---------|----------------|
| **Concept definition** | `Addable` checks that `T + T` exists and returns `T`. |
| **`requires` clause** | `sum`, `Accumulator`, and the generic lambda all use `requires` to enforce the concept. |
| **Automatic error diagnostics** | If you try to instantiate `Accumulator<NoAdd>` (or call `sum` with a non‑addable type), the compiler produces a clear, *concept‑related* error message. |
| **Interoperability** | The same concept works for built‑in types (`int`, `double`) and user‑defined types (`std::string`, any class with `operator+`). |
| **Syntactic elegance** | The `requires` clause keeps the code tidy, replacing verbose `static_assert` checks that were common pre‑C++20. |

Feel free to tweak the `Addable` concept or the `Accumulator` to fit your own domain (e.g. `Sortable`, `Movable`, `CopyConstructible`, etc.). The pattern is the same: declare a concept, use it in a `requires` clause, and the compiler enforces the constraint automatically.
